---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import MiniSim from '../../components/calc/MiniSim';
import YieldChart from '../../components/YieldChart.astro';
import { FUNDS, fund, pct, usd, day, drag, HISTORY, META, TBILLS } from '../../lib/data';
import { src, related } from '../../lib/common';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';
const vmf = fund('VMFXX'), spa = fund('SPAXX'), vms = fund('VMSXX');
const hv = HISTORY.VMFXX;
const first = hv[0], last = hv[hv.length - 1];
const withG = FUNDS.filter((f) => f.g != null);
// Même repère que les pages de fonds : la médiane (testeurs naïfs du 2026-11-10 : « 0.38 % » ici, « 0.42 % » là).
const drags = withG.map((f) => drag(f) ?? 0).sort((a, b) => a - b);
const medDrag = drags[Math.floor(drags.length / 2)];
const eff = (y: number) => (1 + y / 365) ** 365 - 1;
const range = (t: string) => { const h = HISTORY[t]; const ys = h.map((p) => p[1]); return Math.max(...ys) - Math.min(...ys); };
const title = fitTitle(['7-Day Yield of a Money Market Fund: What It Measures', '7-Day Yield of Money Market Funds: What It Measures']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `7-day yield of a money market fund: how it is computed, net against gross, why it trails market rates, and what it pays on your balance, with figures as filed.`,
  `7-day yield of a money market fund: how it is computed, net against gross, why it trails market rates, and what it pays on your own balance, with figures as filed.`,
]);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Is the 7-day yield the same as APY?', a: `No. The 7-day yield is a simple yearly rate: the income of the last seven days, scaled to a year, without interest on interest. An APY (annual percentage yield, the rate banks advertise) counts interest on interest, so it is a little higher. At ${pct(vmf.y)} the APY-style figure is ${pct(eff(vmf.y))}.` },
  { q: 'Why did my money fund yield drop after a Fed cut?', a: `It drops, but not all at once. The fund’s holdings were bought at older rates and are replaced over days and weeks, and the 7-day yield averages a full week of income. A fund whose holdings repay in 40 days on average takes several weeks to follow a rate change fully.` },
  { q: 'Which yield does this site use?', a: `The 7-day yield after fees that each fund reported to the Securities and Exchange Commission (the U.S. markets regulator) for every business day. The latest figures are for the last business day of ${day(META.month).replace(/ \d+,/, '')}, reported between ${day(META.filedFrom)} and ${day(META.filedTo)}. They are not today’s figures.` },
];
---
<GuideLayout lang="en" id="seven-day-yield" title={title} description={description} breadcrumbLabel="7-day yield"
  h1="7-day yield: what a money market fund’s headline number measures"
  intro="The 7-day yield of a money market fund is the one figure every fund publishes. Here it is read from the filings: how it is built, what it leaves out, and what it pays."
  faqs={faqs} related={related('spaxx-vs-fdrxx', 'treasury-bills-vs-money-market-funds', 'money-market-fund-rules')} sources={src('rule-2a7', 'nmfp', 'irs-rp')}>
  {/* Testeurs naïfs du 2026-11-10 : trois sections de texte au plus, une idée par phrase, pas de formule. */}
  <p>The 7-day yield is what a money market fund earned over the past seven days, after its fees, scaled to a year. It is before tax, and it describes the past week, not the next year.</p>

  <div data-simulateur-secondaire><MiniSim client:load kind="seven-day" href="/en/" title={`What a 7-day yield pays on your balance, before tax (SPAXX: ${pct(spa.y)})`} defaults={{ y: +(spa.y * 100).toFixed(2) }} /></div>
  <p>Tax takes part of it. To see what you keep after federal and state tax, use the <a href="/en/">ranking on the home page</a> or a fund’s own page.</p>

  <h2>Net and gross: what fees take</h2>
  <p>The yield a fund publishes is already net of its fees. Each fund also reports what its holdings earned before fees; the gap between the two is what you pay for the fund. SPAXX: {pct(spa.g ?? 0)} before fees, {pct(spa.y)} after, so fees took {pct(drag(spa) ?? 0)}. VMFXX: fees took {pct(drag(vmf) ?? 0)}. The typical fund in the data takes {pct(medDrag)}.</p>

  <h2>Why the yield trails market rates</h2>
  <p>A fund holds bills and short loans bought over the past days and weeks. When the Federal Reserve moves rates, the oldest holdings keep their old rate until they are repaid, so the fund’s yield follows within weeks, not at once. Over the months in the data, VMFXX went from {pct(first[1])} on {day(first[0])} to {pct(last[1])} on {day(last[0])}.</p>
  <YieldChart series={[{ label: 'VMFXX', points: HISTORY.VMFXX, color: '#1F5C4A' }]} caption="VMFXX, daily 7-day yield after fees, as reported by the fund." />

  <h2>Comparing a money fund’s 7-day yield with other rates</h2>
  <p>A bank quotes an APY (annual percentage yield), which counts interest on interest; a 7-day yield does not. At {pct(spa.y)}, SPAXX’s APY-style figure is {pct(eff(spa.y))}. A Treasury bill’s rate is quoted like a 7-day yield: on {day(TBILLS.date)}, the 4-week bill paid {pct(TBILLS.w4)}. <a href="/en/money-market-vs-high-yield-savings/">Savings account against a fund, after tax</a>.</p>

  <details class="not-prose mt-8 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 p-4"><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-navy-900">More detail, for the curious: the formula, fees, other rates</summary>
  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-3 max-w-none">
  <p>How it is built. The formula comes from the Securities and Exchange Commission’s rules for fund advertising: take the change in value of a hypothetical account holding one share at the start of the period, leave out capital gains and losses, divide by the starting value, and multiply by 365/7. Because a money fund keeps its price at $1.00 and pays its income as daily dividends, the result is close to the fund’s income of the week, annualized. On the last filed day, Vanguard Federal (VMFXX) reported {pct(vmf.y)}, which means a week of income at an annual pace of {pct(vmf.y)} of the balance. If short-term rates move, the yield follows within weeks. It is also a simple rate. Dividends credited daily and reinvested earn their own dividends, so over a year a steady {pct(vmf.y)} would grow a balance by about {pct(eff(vmf.y))}, the effective yield.</p>
  <p>Every Form N-MFP report carries two versions of the yield. The gross 7-day yield is what the portfolio earned before the fund deducted its expenses; it is reported once for the whole fund. The net 7-day yield is reported for each share class, after that class’s expenses and any fee waiver. The difference between the two is the cost of owning the class, measured in yield. On the latest filings, Fidelity Government Money Market (SPAXX) had a gross yield of {pct(spa.g ?? 0)} and a net yield of {pct(spa.y)}, a gap of {pct(drag(spa) ?? 0)}; Vanguard Federal had {pct(vmf.g ?? 0)} and {pct(vmf.y)}, a gap of {pct(drag(vmf) ?? 0)}. Across the {withG.length} share classes in the data where both figures are available, the typical (middle) gap was {pct(medDrag)}. Two classes of the same fund hold the same portfolio and show the same gross yield, so any difference in their net yields is cost alone. That is why this site shows the gap, under the name expense drag, on every fund page.</p>
  <p>The expense ratio in a prospectus measures the same thing over a fiscal year. The gap between gross and net yields measures it on one day, and includes waivers in force on that day. When rates were near zero, many funds waived large parts of their fees to keep net yields above zero, and the gap shrank; with rates well above zero, waivers matter much less, and the gap shows the full cost.</p>
  <p>The 7-day yield compares cleanly with a Treasury bill’s coupon-equivalent rate, which the Treasury publishes every business day: both are annualized simple rates on a 365-day year. On {day(TBILLS.date)}, the 4-week bill was at {pct(TBILLS.w4)}. A savings account quotes an APY (annual percentage yield), which compounds; put the two on the same basis before comparing: the calculator above shows the effective version of a 7-day yield, and the savings comparison on this site turns the bank’s APY back into a simple rate, so the fund keeps the figure shown in every ranking. The 30-day SEC yield that bond funds and exchange-traded funds publish is a different measure, based on a month of income and on the yield to maturity of the holdings; it is close to a money fund’s 7-day yield for funds that hold only bills, but the two are not the same calculation.</p>
  <p>None of these rates is what you keep. Money fund dividends are taxed as ordinary income, federally and, except for the share from U.S. obligations, by your state. A fund paying {pct(spa.y)} can leave less after tax than one paying a few hundredths less, depending on what it holds. A municipal fund paying {pct(vms.y)} owes no federal tax and has to be compared on a tax-equivalent basis. That is the purpose of the after-tax yield this site computes from the same filed figures.</p>
  <p>The yields here are the daily 7-day net yields that each fund reported in its monthly Form N-MFP filing, for every business day of the month. The latest month in the data ends on {day(META.month)}; the reports were filed between {day(META.filedFrom)} and {day(META.filedTo)}. A fund company’s own page shows a more recent figure, updated every business day. The two will match on the filed date and drift apart after it. This site uses the filed figure because it is the only one available for every fund on the same date, from the same source, without depending on how each company presents its numbers. On {usd(10000)}, the difference between two funds 10 basis points apart (0.10 percentage point) is {usd(10)} a year before tax: worth knowing, and worth checking against the fund’s current figure before moving money.</p>
  </div>
  </details>
</GuideLayout>
